  - **Still open — the highlighter's own limitation, and it stays true after #771 closed:** this highlighter is **line-based with no cross-line state at all**, so a `/* … */` block comment spanning lines and a string literal spanning lines are both mis-coloured from the second line on. The gap to its sibling widened rather than closed: `significant_braces` now carries **both** facts across lines in a `BraceScanState` — a block-comment **depth** (#771 item 1, closed Day 171) and an open-string delimiter, plain or raw-with-N-hashes (#771 item 2, closed Day 171) — while the highlighter carries neither, so it has no block-comment state of any kind (nested or otherwise) and no open-string state. #771 was about the *scanner that rewrites files*; nothing in it touched this display path, and no fix here is claimed.
  + **Cross-line state (Day 173) — the gap to its sibling is now mostly closed, and the residue is named rather than implied:** this highlighter used to be **line-based with no cross-line state at all**, so a `/* … */` block comment and a string literal spanning lines were both mis-coloured from the second line on, while `significant_braces` carried both facts in a `BraceScanState` (#771 items 1+2, Day 171 — but that issue was about the *scanner that rewrites files* and touched nothing on this display path). Two Day-173 tasks carried the two facts over here into `HighlightState`, which `highlight_code_line_with(lang, line, &mut state)` threads across lines: (a) `block_comment_depth`, a **depth** not a bool, so Rust's nested `/* /* */ */` is one comment while the other C-family languages close on the first `*/`, and a stray `*/` at depth 0 is ordinary code rather than an underflow; (b) `open_string`, a plain `"…"` left open at end of line, set **only** where such a literal really spans lines (`multiline_strings` — Rust today), whose content is emitted string-coloured so `/*`, `//` and keywords inside it are inert and ordinary highlighting resumes just past the closer. `highlight_code_line(lang, line)` is kept as the stateless wrapper and **is** the byte-identity baseline the regression tests assert against: languages with no block comments (python, shell, yaml, toml, json) and every non-Rust language reset the carried flags, and a line with no carried state and no block comment takes a single-segment fast path, so their output is byte-identical to the pre-state behaviour. **Still not carried, deliberately:** Rust **raw** strings (`r#"…"#`) and JS template literals — those still end their run at the line end and so are mis-coloured from line 2; `commands_refactor::significant_braces` remains the only one of the two that models the raw-string delimiter, so the two are still not the same scanner and this is not a claim to lex Rust.
